<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="DE-intro-complete">
    <title>Introduction to Differential Equations (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Schrödinger's equation has motivated most of the topics in our course, and this equation is an example
                        of a differential equation. This is a very broad topic, but we'll work to understand some basics that will 
                        allow us to read and interpret several types of differential equations (DEs).
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
            <title>First Examples</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                The following are all examples of differential equations.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>x'(t) = 2t</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            This could be a model for the velocity of an object at time <m>t</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\frac{dx^2}{dt^2} = -g</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            This is a model for an object in free fall.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>u_t+cu_x = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            This models advection (transport) of a scalar quantity.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = k\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            This is the one-dimensional heat equation.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>u_t = k(u_{xx}+u_{yy})</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            This is the two-dimensional heat equation.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>u_{tt} = c^2u_{xx}</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            This is the wave equation.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\frac{-\overline{h}^2}{2m} \psi'' + V(x)\psi = E\psi</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            This is the time-independent Schrödinger equation.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\frac{-\overline{h}^2}{2m} \psi_{xx} + V(x,t)\psi = i\overline{h} \psi_t</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            This is the time-dependent Schrödinger equation.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise>
        <title>Vocabulary</title>
            <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Ordinary Differential Equation (ODE).
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    An ODE is a differential equation describing a function of a single variable.
                </p>
            </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Partial Differential Equation (PDE).
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    An PDE is a differential equation describing a function of multiple variables.
                </p>
            </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Order.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The order of a DE is the largest order derivative used in the DE.
                </p>
            </solution>
            </task>
             <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   (Spatial) Dimension.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The spatial dimension is the number of variables used to describe space.
                </p>
            </solution>
            </task>
            
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Solutions.</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A <term>solution</term> to a DE is any functions which satisfies the rules of the DE. 
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>y' = 2y</m> has solutions <m>y(x) = Ae^{2x}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Verify that 
                    <md>
                        y'(x) = Ae^{2x} \cdot 2 = 2(Ae^{2x}) = 2y(x)
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\frac{d^2u}{dt^2}+ u =0</m> has solutions <m>u(t) = A\sin(t) + B\cos(t)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Note that 
                    <md>
                        u'(t) = A \cos(t)- B\sin(t)
                    </md>
                    and 
                    <md>
                        u''(t) = -A\sin(t)-B\cos(t)
                    </md>
                    so that <m>u'' + u = 0</m>.
                    
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>u_t + cu_x=0</m> has solutions <m>u(x,t) = f(x-ct)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Verify that 
                    <md>
                        u_t = -cf'(x-ct)
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        u_x = f'(x-ct)
                    </md>
                    so that <m>u_t + cu_x = 0</m>.
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title>General vs. Specific Solutions</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The solutions in the previous examples are <term>general solutions</term>. Without more information, this is the 
                best we can do. The information needed for a <term>specific solution</term> typically comes in one of two forms:
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Boundary Conditions.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    These specify solution behavior at boundary values determined by a spatial domain. For example, consider
                    the 1D Heat equation whose solution function <m>u(x,t)</m> describes the temperature of a rod at
                    position <m>x</m> and time <m>t</m>, for <m>0 \leq x \leq 10</m>. Examples of boundary conditions would be
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>u(0,t) = u(10,t) = 0</m> (<term>Dirichlet conditions</term>)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>u'(0,t) = u'(10,t) = 0</m> (<term>Neumann conditions</term>)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>u(0,t) = u(10,t) </m> (<term>Periodic conditions</term>)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Initial Conditions.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    These specify solution behavior at a specified time, usually <m>t=0</m>. For example, an initial condition
                    for the 1D Heat equation might be <m>u(x,0) = x(x-10)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The number of conditions required is generally determined by
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    the order associated to each variable in the DE. For example, the 1D wave equation is second order in both
                    time and space, and so requires two boundary conditions (for the spatial order) and two initial conditions
                    (for the time order).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Examples: Specific Solutions</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                With boundary/initial conditions, we can determine a specific solution from the general solution.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>y' = 2y</m>, <m>y(0) = 10</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Using the initial condition, we can take the general solution <m>y = A e^{2x}</m> and solve for <m>A=10</m>. 
                    Then the specific solution is <m>y(x) = 10e^{2x}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\frac{d^2u}{dt^2}+ u =0</m>, <m>u(0) 3, u'(0) = -1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We can use the initial data to solve for the coefficients <m>A=-1</m> and <m>B=3</m> in the general solution.
                    So the specific solution is <m>u(t) = -\sin(t) + 3\cos(t)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>
</page>


       
    </worksheet>
